  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x-2){y}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\le 0?$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/3222e808ccb652a4bfb3b3291099b87f5a1db5d4d38635d58c6649d8efc71f06.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/e8ab67f737067fb0e0bfc64a8060fdefacf79ba430bdf9a24d21dbc7893b1cf7.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/42e3960fe067a02d6263df15ede7224ecd6b977c744139889d83529d873096bf.png)",
